            <ArticleTitle>[Efficacy and safety of implantation of multiple drug-eluting stents].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>110-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the efficacy and safety of implantation of multiple drug-eluting stents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective study of 151 cases was conducted, including 34 with implantation of at least 3 drug-eluting stents in the coronary artery (MS group), 53 with implantation of two stents (TS group), and 64 with a single stent (OS group). The incidence of major adverse cardiovascular event (MACE) and restenosis was evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No significant difference was found between the 3 groups in the incidence of MACE or in the stent thrombosis rate 30 days after the implantation. Follow-up of the patients for 6 months still showed comparable restenosis rate and MACE incidence between the 3 groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Implantation of multiple drug-eluting stents does not increase the risk of restenosis or MACE, and has comparable safety and efficacy with implantation of single and two stents.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
